I see that the CSS is in `index.css` (Tailwind-generated) and `styles.css` (source). The `motion-safe:animate-fade-in` class I used needs the `animate-fade-in` to be defined. It's not in the CSS. I have two options:

1. Add `animate-fade-in` to the CSS (I should add it to `index.css` since that's the actual CSS file being used, with all the other utility classes)
2. Replace `motion-safe:animate-fade-in` with inline animation in the JSX

Since the CSS uses a unique approach (hand-written utility classes in index.css + Tailwind), I should add the `fade-in` keyframes + class to `index.css`. Let me add it alongside the existing animations.

Let me add it at around line 877-888 (after `.animate-shimmer`).

```css
@keyframes fade-in {
  from {
    opacity: 0;
  }

  to {
    opacity: 1;
  }
}

.animate-fade-in {
  animation: fade-in 0.2s ease-out forwards;
}
```

Wait, I realized there's an issue. The `motion-safe:` prefix is a Tailwind variant, but this isn't using JIT Tailwind - it's all pre-generated utility classes. Since `motion-safe:animate-fade-in` won't match a Tailwind JIT-generated class, I should just remove the `motion-safe:` prefix. The reduced-motion handling is already done globally in the CSS anyway (lines 1511-1517 in index.css). So `animate-fade-in` alone is fine.

Let me add the fade-in animation to index.css and update the component to use just `animate-fade-in` without `motion-safe:`.

Actually, looking more carefully, the reduced-motion media query at line 1511-1517 already handles all animations by making them instantaneous. So `animate-fade-in` without `motion-safe:` is fine - it'll just be instant for users who prefer reduced motion.

Let me add the animation class to index.css.

